  "narrative_body": "Petr Pavel stood at GLOBSEC and called for NATO to shoot down Russian aircraft, cut Russia off the internet, and sever its banks from global finance — while, at the same meeting, Andrej Babiš's government was being publicly excluded from the alliance's inner circle on Ukraine. NATO Secretary-General Mark Rutte praised Sweden, Germany, the Netherlands, Denmark, Norway, and Canada as \"heavy lifters\" on Ukraine support. Czech Republic was not named.\n\nThe divergence has rarely been so public. Mr Pavel framed Ukraine as \"Europe's defense laboratory\" and said Russia understands \"only the language of power.\" Mr Babiš has maintained he will contribute \"not one crown\" from the state budget to Ukraine arms procurement. Both positions received major international coverage. Czech foreign policy now reads as incoherent to allied audiences — not because the cohabitation is new, but because this week it was more visible than ever before.\n\nEven the outgoing army chief joined the dissent. Karel Řehka, whose successor was named this week, used his final major international platform at GLOBSEC to tell Politico that Ukraine's NATO membership is the \"logical next step\" for European security, and that Czech defense spending must go \"well above 2%\" — a direct contradiction of the government that approved his departure. The resonance is pointed: Mr Řehka's replacement, Deputy Chief of General Staff Miroslav Hlaváč, was chosen by the cabinet over the explicit objection of Defense Minister Jaromír Zůna, who proposed four different candidates and was bypassed on all of them. Mr Zůna was the sole dissenting vote.\n\nThe mechanism behind Mr Hlaváč's appointment tells the broader story. Jakub Landovský, having lost a Defense Ministry strategy post to a rival, was appointed government NATO envoy on May 11. The rival, Jan Šedivý, was dismissed on May 12. Mr Hlaváč was confirmed on May 19. Deník N describes the coalition atmosphere as \"seething.\" Mr Babiš is now concentrating direct defense policy control through Mr Landovský, marginalizing SPD's nominal authority over its own ministry — a pattern confirmed across three consecutive weeks.\n\nThe week's most structurally striking revelation, however, concerned Explosia. During a visit to the company's Pardubice facility, Mr Babiš confirmed that France — through Emmanuel Macron — had expressed interest in buying the firm, with price the only criterion. Explosia is a state-owned manufacturer of Semtex, propellants, and military explosives, and a key link in the Czech and European munitions chain. Mr Babiš himself sold it to the state in 2001–02, describing it as \"a strategic asset essential for state security.\" What the visit also revealed is that Explosia currently has no contractual relationship with the Czech Army — none. Mr Babiš called this \"absurd\" and ordered the company director to fix it urgently. The government is now simultaneously trying to establish Explosia's first-ever army supply contract and floating the firm for foreign sale. Finance Minister Alena Schillerová called it merely \"a commercial idea.\" Critics noted that Mr Babiš is under investigation by French prosecutors over his Côte d'Azur villa — a conflict-of-interest dimension to the French interest that has not gone unnoticed.\n\nThe fiscal confrontation added a further layer of institutional discord. CNB Governor Aleš Michl told a podcast he would \"crush the economy\" before allowing inflation to rise, signalling an upward rate bias confirmed by Bloomberg. He directly identified the source of the pressure: \"Permanent deficits of public finances mean our rates must be far higher than they would be.\" Mr Babiš had publicly pressed the CNB for lower rates, calling the koruna-euro gap \"puzzling.\" The exchange makes plain that Mr Michl, originally Mr Babiš's own appointment to the role, has broken from his political patron — and that the fiscal loosening the government secured last week is now being countered, publicly, by tighter monetary conditions.\n\nInside the coalition, the army chief appointment has set off a retaliatory cycle. Tomio Okamura, the SPD leader, attacked Health Minister Adam Vojtěch — from Mr Babiš's ANO — for accepting an American Ebola patient from Uganda without a cabinet vote, framing it as a security risk. The move was widely read as retaliation for the defense ministry humiliation: SPD is, as one outlet put it, \"losing its voice\" in a government it nominally co-runs. Meanwhile the cabinet quietly approved relocating minority affairs, disability policy, gender equality, civil society cooperation, and human rights portfolios from the Government Office to line ministries — removing cross-cutting oversight visibility from areas where it matters most. The structural anchors holding Czech Republic's Western orientation in place — F-35 procurement, the KHNP nuclear contract, a Senate opposition majority of roughly 59 of 81 seats, and a Constitutional Court composition Mr Pavel has locked in for roughly a decade — remain intact. But the governing coalition has never looked quite so incoherent as it does this week.",
  "other_stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Sudeten German congress in Brno ignites Czech-German relations controversy and coalition rift",
      "summary": "The 76th Sudeten German Congress held in Brno for the first time in Czech history became a major political flashpoint. SPD leader Okamura campaigned against the congress and pushed a parliamentary resolution urging organisers to cancel; ANO largely abstained. President Pavel met Bavarian PM Söder on the sidelines, while Finance Minister Schillerová refused to meet Söder separately, citing an inflamed atmosphere. Czech writer Milan Uhde received the Sudeten Germans' highest award. The episode strained Czech-Bavarian and broader Czech-German relations, with German media and EU partners critical of the coalition's posture."
    },
    {
      "headline": "ANO sidelines SPD in Chief of General Staff appointment, fuelling coalition tensions over defense ministry",
      "summary": "A significant coalition rift emerged after PM Babiš bypassed SPD's defense minister Jaromír Zůna to appoint Miroslav Hlaváč as new Chief of the General Staff — overriding all four candidates Zůna had proposed. Zůna was the only cabinet member to vote against. Reports described repeated tense meetings between Babiš and Zůna over army reform concepts, and the appointment of Jakub Landovský as government envoy for NATO commitments without consulting SPD. Opposition figures including ODS euroMP Vondra called for Zůna's resignation; SPD defended the minister. Babiš was described as increasingly asserting direct control over defence policy."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Babiš-Pavel standoff deepens over NATO Ankara summit delegation",
      "summary": "The ongoing constitutional dispute between President Pavel and PM Babiš over who may attend the July NATO summit in Ankara as part of the official Czech delegation came to a head this week. Babiš has insisted the president cannot be part of the government delegation, while Pavel argues he has traditionally represented the country at summits and even offered to self-fund his attendance. Politico described a rocky relationship with repeated clashes over foreign policy, democratic norms and business conflicts of interest. Foreign Minister Macinka proposed delaying the decision until after the 12 June NATO defence ministers' meeting."
    },
    {
      "headline": "Okamura breaks with coalition over Ebola patient transfer, deepening ANO-SPD tensions",
      "summary": "SPD leader and Parliamentary Speaker Tomio Okamura publicly attacked Health Minister Adam Vojtěch (ANO) for accepting an American patient with suspected Ebola from Uganda on US request, treating it as a security risk and criticising the lack of government vote on the decision. The dispute was widely read as an escalation of broader ANO-SPD tensions following the army chief debacle. Commentators noted Okamura had long targeted Vo"
    }
  ]
}

